A||||9||lou_dias||4 wheel drive||||1||
Z||000000||lou_dias||10-24-2000||08:15 PM||||This might be a stupid question...but...<BR>Say you had a Mitsubishi 3000 GT VR4 and you mounted the starter on the other side of the engine and rewired the spark plug wires so it spins and fires backwards...  Would the transmission be able to spin the other way and remain stable and useable?<P>Are transmissions made to spin only one way when it comes to bolting up to the flywheel?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-24-2000||08:34 PM||||That would depend on how the tranny gears are cut. It would only work for a stick even then. Automatics always have a directional preference. No other way to make them really.<P>You'd need a new cam for the motor. You'll also have cam drive issues. Belt drives are almost always built to only run in one direction. Chain sets often have the same problem. Gears might not care.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Terrybogin||10-24-2000||09:02 PM||||Back in my youth and foolishness, I ran a late model modified at Talledega. We bought a "reverse rotation" kit from Smokey Y. and ran it for a while- until everybody figured out what was going on and a rules change kept us from using it anymore. It came with a cam, starter, timing set, distributer gear and required that the rear end gears be flipped around so the ring was on the opposite side of the pinion. It was pretty cool to have our chasis load the left side under acceleration. . . Oh, to be young again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||10-24-2000||10:52 PM||||Smokey Yunick is one of my heros. What a ornary ol' cuss, but man, could he squeak out horsepower in ways nobody else had ever thought about.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lou_dias||10-25-2000||12:18 PM||||Well, I was just wondering the feasability of taking my brother's car with a blown tranny and flipping it around and mounting a Fiero on it...  300 Twin-Turboed factory HP.  The engine has 90k miles on it.<BR>Any swaps for that motor into a Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000005||sparks||10-25-2000||12:54 PM||||I know I've heard of it being done in VW's.<P>some guys on the Vintage VW list w/ me have talked about putting Corvair engines in VW's, you have to make it run backwards to work right w/ a Bug transaxle.  But then that wasn't computer controlled.<P>I don't know that would be the only thing I could think of that might cause a glitch.<P>------------------<BR>--<BR>sparks||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||10-25-2000||03:13 PM||||When a "mid engine" layout is used in a VW rail type buggy, the transaxle is behind the engine. To make this work, the differential is dissassembled and the carrier is installed on the other side (Like Terrybogin's race car)This could also be done for the corvair VW to keep from getting into weird engine stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000007||alpha||10-25-2000||05:34 PM||||They do both methods to put the Corvair engines in ACVW's. Either the different cam or flipping the ring and pinnion will work.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||EdsB52||10-26-2000||02:25 AM||||It can be done, but you are legally required to change the make of the car on the title to a Firiceo.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||10-26-2000||03:14 PM||||Oncest upon a time, my dad had a mid-engine corvair with a 365 horse 327.<BR>     The kit included a replacement for the front of the corvair transaxle that allowed a bell housing to be bolted to it.<P>One might be able to use a VR4 or GSX drivetrain with a subaru or AMC eagle front axle, but the engine would have to be behind the rear axle. The car would no longer be mid-engine. Is that an iron block engine? and iron block engine behind the rear axle would make the car entirely too tail-happy, methinks.||||reg||1
